# Backslashes in a normal string are escapes; a Windows path gets a verbatim-string hint.
mode: syntax
---- query
from 'C:\data\x.csv' | take 5
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..30
  PIPELINE@0..29
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    LITERAL@5..20
      STRING@5..20 "'C:\\data\\x.csv'"
    WHITESPACE@20..21 " "
    PIPE@21..22 "|"
    WHITESPACE@22..23 " "
    TAKE_OP@23..29
      IDENT@23..27 "take"
      WHITESPACE@27..28 " "
      LITERAL@28..29
        LONG@28..29 "5"
  WHITESPACE@29..30 "\n"

error[DS0005]: unknown escape sequence `\d`
  --> query:1:9
  |
1 | from 'C:\data\x.csv' | take 5
  |         ^^   -- `\x` is not valid either
  |         |
  |         not a valid escape
  |
  = help: if this is a Windows path, use a verbatim string `@'C:\data\x.csv'` or forward slashes `'C:/data/x.csv'`
  = note: valid escapes are `\\`, `\"`, `\'`, `\n`, `\r`, `\t`, `\0` and `\u{...}`
